          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My son has been wheezing at night for a month and his blue inhaler is running out fast. Should he be seen urgently, and what does asthma guidance describe about stepping up treatment?",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now if your son has any of these:**\n- Breathing that is fast, or that is hard even at rest\n- Trouble speaking in full sentences, or pausing for breath between words\n- Blue or grey lips, tongue, face or fingertips\n- Drowsiness, confusion, or exhaustion\n- Skin sucking in between or under the ribs, grunting, or flaring nostrils\n- A chest that has gone quiet, or wheezing that stops while he is still struggling to breathe\n- No improvement after his reliever inhaler, or symptoms coming back within a few hours\n\nThese signs come from the emergency guidance in the red-flag document (\"Severe difficulty breathing\") and the Asthma condition summary.\n\n**If none of those apply**, the tool did not flag his description as an emergency. That does not mean it is safe to wait. A month of night-time wheezing, with his blue reliever running out faster, should be discussed with a doctor soon, not left until a routine check. The Asthma summary says an attack that does not respond to reliever treatment needs prompt assessment. The adherence document says rising reliever use is a sign the condition may not be under control. Call his doctor or a nurse line this week, and ask whether he needs to be seen sooner.\n\n**On stepping up treatment:** the guideline document (\"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach\") says that reliever-only treatment is no longer considered appropriate at any severity. It says heavy reliever use is a risk marker in itself. It also describes treatment as a series of steps, chosen from current symptom control and past flare-ups, and reviewed over time. The passage I received cuts off before it lists the individual steps, so I cannot tell you what those steps are or what would suit him. The document also notes that many people with an asthma label have not had it confirmed by testing. His doctor can decide whether a change is needed, and which one. Do not change or stop any inhaler without advice from his doctor.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
